ENTANGLEMENT, CLASSICAL vs QUANTUM vs RELATIONAL
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TYPES
  Classical:  sinusoidal traveling waves, continuous metric propagation
  Quantum:    non-factorizable Hilbert-space states, Bell-violating correlations
  RELATIONAL:        structural geometry with Q-boundary decomposition

VARIABLES (CLASSICAL)
  θ            : phase angle
  v(θ)         : instantaneous voltage
  V_peak       : peak amplitude
  V_rms        : root-mean-square amplitude
  sin(θ)       : sine function

CLASSICAL FUNDAMENTALS
  v(θ) = V_peak · sin(θ)
  V_rms = V_peak / √2 ≈ 0.707 V_peak

  Correlation mechanism:
    f(θ) = correlation by shared past + continuous propagation
    Waves travel WEST/EAST from a common ripple origin
    Alice and Bob sample v(θ) at different spatial positions

STANDARD QUANTUM FUNDAMENTALS
  |ψ⟩          : joint state in tensor-product Hilbert space
  ρ            : density operator
  Entanglement : non-factorizability of |ψ⟩ or ρ
  Bell violation: no local hidden-variable model reproduces correlations

  Correlation mechanism:
    Non-local correlations from structure of |ψ⟩
    No continuous metric propagation required
    No classical shared past required

RELATIONAL FUNDAMENTALS
  S            : full structural geometry (S-mode)
  Q            : discontinuous boundary operator
  A            : Q-invariant relational stratum
  S \ A        : Q-unstable geometry

  Formal identity:
    A = S_invariant^Q

  Structural decomposition:
    S = A ⊕ (S \ A)

  Correlation mechanism:
    g(A) = correlation by shared A-stratum
    A survives Q-boundary projection
    A is re-instantiable without continuous propagation

DISTINCTIONS
  Classical:          f(θ) — correlation by shared past, continuous metric
  Standard quantum:   non-factorizable |ψ⟩ — Bell-violating, no propagation
  RELATIONAL:                g(A) — correlation by Q-invariant stratum

  Classical: phase alignment (θ)
  Quantum:  entangled-state outcome correlation
  RELATIONAL:      A-stratum alignment

SUMMARY
  Classical diagram shows local, metric, propagation-based correlation.
  Standard quantum entanglement shows nonlocal, Bell-violating correlation
    from non-factorizable Hilbert-space states.
  RELATIONAL provides a structural decomposition:
    A = S_invariant^Q
  identifying the Q-stable relational geometry responsible for nonlocal
  correlations, without deriving them from Hilbert-space machinery.
